The patch has runtime migration and memory-regression issues in the compression level/window handling, plus the project setting meant to disable analysis is ineffective. These are actionable defects introduced by the change set.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Avoid forcing 8 MiB windows for all levels — ./zstd-IIS/src/zstd.c:22-22
  With the documented 104/107 settings (zstd levels 4/7), zstd's defaults use a smaller window, but `ZSTD_c_windowLog = 23` overrides the value rather than capping it. Since this patch also rejects levels above 17, accepted levels no longer need a window above Chrome's 8 MiB limit, so lower-level responses now advertise/allocate an 8 MiB window unnecessarily and increase per-request memory under concurrency.

- [P2] Preserve legacy positive level values — ./zstd-IIS/src/zstd.c:66-66
  When upgrading from the base release, existing IIS configs that followed the previous README can have values like `30`/`35` for positive zstd levels; this remapping now turns those into `-30`/`-35`, and the lower-bound check rejects them, so those deployments stop compressing until the config is manually edited. Either accept the old `23..45` range as aliases or document the full breaking migration range.

- [P3] Disable PREfast when turning off analysis — ./zstd-IIS/src/zstdIIS.vcxproj:49-49
  Setting `RunCodeAnalysis` to false doesn't actually stop `/analyze` while the project still has `<EnablePREfast>true</EnablePREfast>` under `ClCompile`; builds through the new script still invoke native code analysis, so the advertised faster build path remains slow and can depend on analysis components being installed. Remove or set `EnablePREfast` to false as part of this change.
